  {"t":"h2","text":"Day 1: Hamra, AUB & the Corniche"},
  {"t":"p","html":"Start in <strong>Hamra</strong>, Ras Beirut's dense, bustling commercial spine and the most walkable base in the city. Breakfast is a foul-and-hummus counter, not a hotel buffet: <strong>Abu Hassan Hummus &amp; Fool</strong> is open 24 hours if you're up early, or <strong>Malek al Foul</strong> — 'the King of Foul,' hand-mashing the same recipe since 1965 — a few streets over near the American University of Beirut."},
  {"t":"p","html":"Spend the late morning wandering Hamra itself: AUB's leafy campus, the secondhand bookshops, and a coffee stop at one of the cafés the neighborhood is known for. It's dense and a little frayed at the edges at almost any hour — that's the character, not a flaw."},
  {"t":"h3","text":"Golden hour on the Corniche"},
  {"t":"p","html":"Late afternoon, walk (or Bolt) down to the <strong>Corniche</strong>, Beirut's 4.8km seafront promenade — free, self-guided, and best at golden hour with the whole city out for its evening stroll. Follow it west toward <strong>Raouché</strong> for a clifftop view of the <strong>Pigeon Rocks</strong>, Lebanon's only natural sea arches, right as the light turns."},
  {"t":"p","html":"For dinner, stay in Hamra: <strong>Taht El Shajra</strong> ('Under the Tree') runs breakfast through dinner with Arabic coffee and inventive plates, or grab a manoushe from <strong>Abu Shadi</strong> — no sign on the street, reportedly the best in the neighborhood — if you want something lighter before an early night."},
  {"t":"h2","text":"Day 2: museums by day, Mar Mikhael by night"},
  {"t":"p","html":"Pick one museum for the morning rather than rushing both — they sit on opposite sides of the city. The <strong>Beirut National Museum</strong> in Badaro holds around 1,300 Phoenician-to-Mamluk artifacts, including the Byblos figurines, plus the quietly remarkable story of how staff hid and protected the collection through the civil war. The <strong>Sursock Museum</strong> in Achrafieh covers modern and contemporary art across 800-plus works, with the still-private, gated Sursock Palace visible right across the street. Both are self-ticketed at the door."},
  {"t":"p","html":"Early afternoon, head to <strong>Beirut Souks &amp; Downtown</strong> around Martyrs' Square — the rebuilt heart of the city, 200-plus shops in a polished, walkable streetscape — and the blue-domed <strong>Mohammad Al-Amin Mosque</strong> next door, which stands beside the Maronite Cathedral of Saint George, a pairing locals point to as a symbol of the city's coexistence."},
  {"t":"h3","text":"Evening: Mar Mikhael & Gemmayzeh"},
  {"t":"p","html":"Cross town for the night to <strong>Mar Mikhael</strong>, Beirut's liveliest bar strip after dark, or one flight up the <strong>Saint Nicolas Stairs</strong> to <strong>Gemmayzeh</strong>'s French-mandate townhouses and street art for a calmer version of the same energy. If you want the city's most talked-about table, <strong>LMATBAKH</strong> in Mar Mikhael runs a live chef's-counter tasting menu over two nightly seatings only — book ahead, it's dine-in only. Otherwise, the strip's grills and bars need no reservation at all."},
